> quota admission is supposed to ignore resources that are not being increased by the item being created/updated... if the value of the nodeport resource is computed to be zero for the service, it should have been allowed

Yeah, quota evaluation did not correctly handle an evaluator returning a resource list that mixed zero and non-zero usage. 

This fix would resolve the "new object not using quota'ed resource X is blocked by excess usage of resource X by existing objects":

```diff
diff --git a/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/resourcequota/controller.go b/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/resourcequota/controller.go
index 622c7bcfd50..fa6ec8ae781 100644
--- a/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/resourcequota/controller.go
+++ b/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/resourcequota/controller.go
@@ -510,7 +510,14 @@ func CheckRequest(quotas []corev1.ResourceQuota, a admission.Attributes, evaluat
 		}
 	}
 
-	if quota.IsZero(deltaUsage) {
+	// ignore items in deltaUsage with zero usage
+	for k, v := range deltaUsage {
+		if v.IsZero() {
+			delete(deltaUsage, k)
+		}
+	}
+	// if there is no remaining non-zero usage, short-circuit and return
+	if len(deltaUsage) == 0 {
 		return quotas, nil
 	}
```

We need an accompanying unit test that verifies this case (evaluator returns a mix of zero and non-zero usage, and zero usage is ignored).

The changes in this PR look reasonable as well (and resolve a separate bug with the service evaluator not computing node port usage correctly), but I think we should include both fixes.

